        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Plain aluminum does not work on induction, and no amount of fiddling with settings will change that. What works is aluminum with magnetic steel attached: a bonded plate, a pressed disc or a clad exterior. A fridge magnet in the center of the base tells you which you have in a few seconds.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Keep the pans that pass, use a converter disk only for the rare favorite that does not, and replace the everyday pieces that fail. A small set of induction-ready pans will do more for your cooking than a drawer full of converted ones.
          </p>
